WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.372
Two Pandas objects appear throughout this module: Series and DataFrame.

00:00:05.372 --> 00:00:09.097
A Series is essentially one labeled dimension of data.

00:00:09.097 --> 00:00:12.732
You can think of it as one column together with an index.

00:00:12.732 --> 00:00:20.032
For example, if we select only the amount column from an orders table, Pandas usually returns a Series.

00:00:20.032 --> 00:00:24.487
A DataFrame is a two-dimensional table with rows and columns.

00:00:24.487 --> 00:00:29.097
Each column has a name, and each column also has a data type.

00:00:29.097 --> 00:00:36.002
This distinction matters because some operations behave differently depending on whether you are working with

00:00:36.002 --> 00:00:38.632
a Series or a DataFrame.

00:00:38.632 --> 00:00:43.827
For example, orders bracket amount returns one column and usually gives us a Series.

00:00:43.827 --> 00:00:51.862
If we place amount inside a list and use double brackets, Pandas returns a DataFrame containing one column.

00:00:51.862 --> 00:00:57.292
This may look like a small difference, but it explains many common beginner errors.

00:00:57.292 --> 00:01:02.012
A useful habit is to ask: what kind of object do I have right now?

00:01:02.012 --> 00:01:06.062
If you are unsure, use type and inspect the result.

00:01:06.062 --> 00:01:11.282
Understanding the object you are working with makes the rest of Pandas much easier.
